{
  "image_name": "c034140be2151e82c8f24bd221211668.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how solid-state battery interfaces degrade during cycling in two different cell designs. In both cases, the left image shows an initially intact structure and the right image shows damage after repeated charge–discharge.\n\nIn the upper scheme, a high-voltage cathode is in contact with an inorganic solid-state electrolyte and a metal anode. Cycling appears to create a cathode–electrolyte interfacial layer, with large volume changes near the cathode, high-voltage decomposition, and cracking through grain boundaries in the electrolyte. The cracks and newly conductive reaction products allow electrons and lithium ions to penetrate undesired regions, increasing short-circuit risk. At the metal anode side, additional interfacial reactions form an SEI-like layer.\n\nIn the lower scheme, a composite cathode using a solid polymer electrolyte binder initially forms a relatively continuous contact with the metal anode side above. After cycling, the polymer-related interphase becomes unstable, dendrite growth emerges from the metal anode, and the cathode region loses integrity and becomes pulverized. Overall, the figure highlights that mechanical fracture, chemically formed interphases, electronic leakage, and dendrite formation are coupled failure mechanisms that limit solid-state battery stability.",
